A||||14||Nashco||Yet ANOTHER 17" wheel/tire question...I know...||||1||
Z||000000||Nashco||11-04-2001||04:26 AM||||Alright, I have looked through everything in the search function that had "17" in it, so I think I covered that base.  I am about to put some money down on the wheels I've been wanting to get, but need the forum's approval first <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  The credit card company decided I deserve a higher limit, and seeing as I just paid it off.....I have to try out that limit, make sure I really can go over my old one!<P>I am planning on buying these wheels:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.washingtontire.com/images/tsw/vx1.jpg"> <P>I am getting 17x7 with 40mm offset in front, and 17x8 with 40mm offset in rear.<P>For tires I want to use 215/45 on the front and 235/45 on the rear.  I'm planning on buying Kumho Ecsta Supra 712s from tirerack.com.  <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com/tires/kumho/ku_712.jsp" TARGET=_blank>Check them out here</A>.<P>So, the price I've got on the wheels is about 825 including shipping and lugnuts.  The price on the tires is about 420 including shipping.<P>What do you guys think?  Do you think this offset will work well on the 88?  Should I go for it, search for a better deal, get a different size, get a different brand/style????  I contacted the wheel dealer, and I plan on calling him Tuesday with confirmation and a credit card bill if all is well.<P>Please keep in mind that this is a HUGE expense for me (po' college boy), so spending another four or five hundred bucks for added performance isn't an option.  I've done a ton of searching, and this is the best performance I think I can afford.  I'd love to hear if there are more affordable deals out there, or much better performance within a couple hundred bucks.<P>I value what a lot of the people on the forum think, and I'd like your opinion on this.  Thanks <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||11-04-2001||06:45 AM||||Try <A HREF="http://www.whalentire.com" TARGET=_blank>Whalen Tire</A> first.  I know a forum member and an ex member who have purchased wheel/tire combos from them with good results and good prices.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||11-04-2001||11:22 AM||||Thanks for the tip Monkeyman.  They do have some good prices on wheels, but I didn't see any wheels with the sizes I'm looking for in a style I liked.  I'm trying to have the wider rear wheel, so I can have a larger rear tire without having the sidewalls be oriented differently.<P>FYI for anybody looking around, they have some Konig SubZero 17x7.5 wheels that cost 130 bucks and only weigh 16 pounds, they say.  That is a great deal on such a lightweight wheel!  I'm almost tempted to drop my different wheel size criteria and get them....sooooo many choices!!!<P>Anybody else have some recommendations/suggestions?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||11-04-2001||12:22 PM||||Hey, I bought a set of 16 inch wheels with Kumho Ecsta Supra tires not too long ago from the TireRack. They are great, very fast shipping and quite responsive.<P>The Kumho Ecsta Supra tires are a super deal. The handling you get from them is incredible, they look good, and they are a great price. I was VERY happy with them. I had 225/50-16 rear and 205/55-16 front,my 88 coupe was handling like an exotic supercar with them. With 45 series on a 17 inch wheel, you should be stuck to the road like glue  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Those wheels look pretty nice also! Good luck with them.||||reg||14
Z||000004||TRiAD||11-04-2001||01:33 PM||||Advice;<P>1) NEVER "charge" mods to your car. <P>2) Those wheels stick out quite a bit, TSWs offsets are never right, and TSWs are HEAVY.<P>You asked.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0NgAlAL0S2vLvViYU9P7uloj72fLFTqfz59trng8I7AOG7hGCvfk6WP96n!gnOaEUAvooy1kUoWucv7BJ7L5EWg/temp-sig.gif">    <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rick 88||11-04-2001||05:40 PM||||Do the spokes on those wheels stick out past the rim? I saw a new Neon with a very similar factory wheel and the spokes were visible when viewed from the side. Be extra careful with curbs if they do. You shoud also check to see if the tires have a "rim protector bead" My Falken Ziex ZE-502's have this feature to help protect against curb rash.<P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||11-04-2001||09:03 PM||||Master_Sushi:  About 90% of the ratings on tirerack.com gave the tire rave reviews, as you have, so I feel quite confident about them.<P>Triad:  I saw you post bad omen warnings about the TSWs before.  Are the offsets usually greater than/less than stated?  I know they're heavy...the 17s are weighing in at almost 25 pounds per wheel!!  I'm really considering those other wheels I mentioned above, the Konig SubZero.  I wanted the staggered wheel sizes, but that weight is VERY low, and performance improvements would definitely be noted.  All for a reduction in price too!  I am in love with the VX1 look, but I'll sacrifice a little style for a lot of performance any day!  What experience can you pass on to me about the TSW and Konig?  I've heard good things about both from the few people that I've talked to, but haven't talked with anybody that has a lot of experience to draw from.<P>Rick88: Yes, the spokes stick out past the face of the wheel just a tiny bit, looks like less than .5" in the pictures.  Curbs aren't an issue with the tires or wheels though, I avoid them like the plague as it is.  If my wheels and/or tires come in contact with a curb, it means I misjudged the corner, he ran me off the road, and either way, I lose <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>If I get 4 17x7.5 instead of staggered wheel sizes, what should I got with?  225/45 or 215/40?  I'm thinking the 225/45 would work out best (gear ratio, speedo, contact patch, etc.)<P>Thanks for your help, I have a really hard time making decisions when I'm spending so much money...if I was rich it wouldn't be such an issue  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||11-04-2001||10:41 PM||||Go 245/40/17 in the rear (with 215/45/17 fronts) That'll be perfect, as the rear will only be slightly taller than the front, not MUCH taller as with /45 series in back.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000008||Nashco||11-05-2001||12:00 PM||||Alright, did a little change in plans due to your recommendations.  I'm dropping the VX1, and decided to go with a Konig Tantrum.  Konig's have a pretty good reputation, these come in 7" and 8" sizes (even 9" if I want those later down the road), they're lighter, and they cost about 25 bucks each less.  Here's a pic:<P>I asked a friend who is up on stuff, and he agreed with Ben.  245/40 in the rear and 215/45 up front.  I'm still sticking with the Kumho 712s.  This package deal is about 1150 including shipping from discounttiredirect.com<P>So, I'm about THIS---&gt; &gt;&lt; CLOSE to making a deal.  I'm waiting on a couple other quotes.<P>Anybody have objections to Konig Tantrums?<P>Thanks for your help guys.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
